// cursor.8bs's own `.web` twin (build.mjs adds `web` to a real machine's
// own --web build's tags, the same twin mechanism a hardware tag like
// `keys.pet.business.8bs` already uses, for the wasm backend instead of a
// hardware option). No JSR $FFF0 here: that address is the VIC-20 KERNAL's
// own PLOT routine, and a wasm build of this package has no KERNAL, no
// BASIC, and — unlike the native build cursor.8bs serves — nothing that
// ever hands control back to one. releaseCursor()'s entire job on real
// hardware is making BASIC's *next* PRINT land in the right place once
// the program returns; a wasm build never returns to a BASIC prompt at
// all, so there is no next PRINT for this to matter to, on this target,
// ever. A true no-op, not a workaround for one that failed — unlike
// packages/pet/src/text.8bs's own releaseCursor(), which is empty because
// a real fix was tried on real PET hardware and measured not to work.
export function releaseCursor(): void {
}
